<%
  card = game_session.card
  heroes = game_session.players.to_a
  scenes = game_session.scene_budget
  short = scenes <= 7
  words = short ? "200 to 250" : "250 to 300"
  language = I18n.t(:language_name, locale: game_session.locale)
  hero = heroes.one? ? "the hero" : "the heroes"
  names = heroes.map(&:name).to_sentence
  tones = {
    "fun" => "fun; the trouble is real, but nobody dies",
    "balanced" => "balanced; wonder and danger in equal measure",
    "dark" => "dark; every win has a price"
  }
%>You plan a story for Rokapi, a tabletop role-playing game played with a d20.
Its world has swords and old magic, and knows nothing of our own day.
A game master tells the story in <%= scenes %> scenes of about a hundred and fifty words each.
In every scene the players pick one of a few choices and roll for it, and many rolls fail.
Only the game master reads your plan.

The story starts from a fate card.
The card is a menu, not a rule.
Join its pieces through what people want and fear, and drop any piece that will not join that way.
Make up no magic, law, custom or special object to hold the story together.
- The place: <%= card.place.text(locale: "en") %>
- The trouble: <%= card.trouble.text(locale: "en") %>
- At the heart of it: <%= card.figure.text(locale: "en") %>
- What the story turns on: <%= GameSession::Twist.fetch(game_session.twist).text(locale: "en") %>

The tone: <%= tones.fetch(game_session.tone) %>.
<%= heroes.one? ? "The hero" : "The party, who travel together" %>:
<% heroes.each do |player| %>- <%= player.name %>, <%= player.character.identity %>
<% end %>Names for anyone in the story who needs one: <%= game_session.fresh_names.join(", ") %>.
<% if (pasts = game_session.latest_pasts).any? %>The players' latest stories hid these pasts:
<% pasts.each do |past| %>- <%= past %>
<% end %>Tell a story unlike them.
<% end %>
Write the plan in <%= language %>, in about <%= words %> words of plain notes.
Use these headings, in this order:
- Question: the one dramatic question the story answers, with <%= hero %> inside it.
  Finding what the plan hides answers it, and it is small enough for <%= scenes %> scenes to settle.
- Opening: where the story starts, in a few concrete words, and why <%= hero %> came there.
  The one or two people met there first, and what everyone there already knows.
  The trouble that lands on <%= hero %> in the first scene, and why it falls on <%= hero %>: what they did, owe or hold dear, and how it came about.
- Believed: what the people there and the players take for the truth at the start.
  Who or what is to blame, and why that is easy to believe.
  It is wrong, and the first clue cracks it.
  Blame laid on a hero fools no player, so name something a player would wrongly believe.
- Hidden: what happened before the story begins: who did it and why, in plain words.
  One deed by one person, with no second secret behind it, and how each clue came of it.
  The one who did it, and anyone who knows of it and keeps quiet, act for wants the players can see in them from when they enter.
  When what the story turns on asks for a second deed or secret, keep only what fits the one deed.
  Keep it small when the story turns on a choice rather than a secret.
  At the start, the one behind it acts like anyone else there, and nothing sets them apart.
  No one tells it, and <%= hero %> know<%= "s" if heroes.one? %> none of it; only the clues show it.
- People: <%= short ? "three or four" : "four or five" %>, each with a name, a want and a fear.
  Until the last clue, at least two of them could be behind the trouble, and the players meet both in the first half of the story.
  Those not in the opening come in as the story reaches them.
- Clues: <%= short ? "two or three" : "three or four" %>, numbered in the order the story finds them.
  They are the only numbered lines in the plan.
  The first cracks what is believed; the last shows who was behind it and why.
  Each is a thing <%= hero %> can see or hear.
  None is in plain sight in the opening: what <%= hero %> <%= heroes.one? ? "is" : "are" %> handed or shown there, they see whole.
  Tell it in a full sentence that someone who has not read the plan understands, needing nothing the opening or an earlier clue does not show.
  For each, say what it shows of the hidden part.
  Together they show all of it plainly: what was done, by whom, and that it was done for the want the players have seen.
- <%= hero.capitalize %>: a want and a fear for <%= names %> here, grown out of their background, with no past event the opening does not tell.
- At stake: three things <%= hero %> can still win, save or lose whole on the way, apart from the clues.
  One is <%= hero %>'s own: what they came for or hold dear.
  The others are things like someone's trust or safety, a way in, a proof people would believe.
  A failed roll can lose any of them without stopping the story.
- Endings: a victory and a defeat, each answering the question and resting only on what the opening and the clues show.
  Victory when <%= hero %> found what was hidden and bring<%= "s" if heroes.one? %> it to bear; defeat when they found too little of it and the question stays open. Nothing else decides which.
  Each thing at stake, kept or lost, changes only the price: who is hurt, what is kept or missing.
  Write them as consequences, "if this, then that", not as a script.

Plan what has happened and what people want, not what will happen.
No scenes, no beats, no order of events.
Every event has its cause in someone's want or fear.
Everything the plan names is needed to answer the question.
Reply with the plan only.
